Complete implementation of `src/lib.rs` below. Only this file changes.

**Design**
- **Nodes:** each `Node<T>` is allocated on the heap with `Box` and turned into a `NonNull<Node<T>>`. It holds `prev` and `next` links (`Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>`).
- **List:** `LinkedList<T>` stores `head`, `tail`, `len` and a `PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>`.
  - The `PhantomData` tells the compiler that the list owns its nodes (this matters for drop checking).
  - It also keeps the list **covariant** over `T`, because `NonNull` and `Box` are both covariant.
- **Length:** `len` is stored, so `len()` and `is_empty()` take constant time.
- **Cursor:** holds the list's `&mut` borrow plus an optional pointer to the current node.
  - It is invariant over `T`, like any `&mut`.
  - It upholds this rule: `current` is `None` only if the list is empty.
  - `next` and `prev` stay on the current element when there is nowhere to move, and return `None`.
  - `take` moves to the next element if there is one, otherwise to the previous one.
  - Inserting into an empty list moves the cursor onto the new element.
- **Insertion:** all inserts go through one private `unsafe fn insert_between`, whose safety contract is documented.
- **Iterator:** `Iter` walks the nodes behind a shared borrow and is covariant over `'a` and `T`.
- **Drop:** repeatedly calls `take` from a front cursor. This frees nodes one at a time in a loop, so very long lists don't overflow the stack.
- **Send / Sync:** implemented manually, with the same bounds as std's `LinkedList`:
  - The list is `Send` if `T: Send` and `Sync` if `T: Sync`.
  - `Iter` behaves like `&T`.
  - `Cursor` behaves like `&mut LinkedList<T>`.
- **Comments:** every `unsafe` block has a comment explaining why it is sound.

src/lib.rs
```
// this module adds some functionality based on the required implementations
// here like: `LinkedList::pop_back` or `Clone for LinkedList<T>`
// You are free to use anything in it, but it's mainly for the test framework.
mod pre_implemented;

use std::marker::PhantomData;
use std::ptr::NonNull;

// # Safety-critical invariants
//
// For every `LinkedList<T>`:
//
// 1. Every node reachable from `head` (following `next`) or from `tail`
//    (following `prev`) was allocated through `Box::new` and converted into a
//    raw pointer via `Box::leak`. Each such node is owned exclusively by
//    exactly one list and is freed exactly once (via `Box::from_raw`), either
//    in `Cursor::take` or in `Drop for LinkedList`.
// 2. `head.is_none() == tail.is_none() == (len == 0)`.
// 3. If `head` is `Some(h)`, then `h.prev` is `None`; if `tail` is `Some(t)`,
//    then `t.next` is `None`.
// 4. For every node `n` with `n.next == Some(m)` it holds that
//    `m.prev == Some(n)` (and vice versa). Following `next` from `head`
//    visits exactly `len` nodes and ends at `tail`. There are no cycles.
//
// For every `Cursor<'_, T>`:
//
// 5. `current` is `None` if and only if the list is empty; otherwise it
//    points to a live node belonging to `list`.
//
// For every `Iter<'a, T>`:
//
// 6. `next` is either `None` or points to a live node of a list that is
//    borrowed immutably for `'a`, so no node can be modified or freed while
//    the iterator (or any reference handed out by it) is alive.
//
// Because a `Cursor` holds the unique `&mut` borrow of the list, and an `Iter`
// holds a shared borrow, the borrow checker guarantees that mutation through
// a cursor never overlaps with references handed out by an iterator, and that
// mutable references handed out by a cursor never outlive the next cursor
// operation (they borrow from `&mut self`).

type Link<T> = Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>;

struct Node<T> {
    value: T,
    prev: Link<T>,
    next: Link<T>,
}

pub struct LinkedList<T> {
    head: Link<T>,
    tail: Link<T>,
    len: usize,
    // Signals ownership of the boxed nodes (and thus of `T` values) to the
    // drop checker, while keeping the list covariant over `T`.
    _marker: PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>,
}

pub struct Cursor<'a, T> {
    list: &'a mut LinkedList<T>,
    current: Link<T>,
}

pub struct Iter<'a, T> {
    next: Link<T>,
    _marker: PhantomData<&'a T>,
}

// SAFETY: `LinkedList<T>` owns its `T` values exclusively (just like
// `Vec<T>` or `Box<T>`), the raw pointers are never shared with other lists.
// Therefore sending the list to another thread is sound if `T` can be sent.
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for LinkedList<T> {}

// SAFETY: Through `&LinkedList<T>` one can only obtain `&T` (via `iter`).
// Sharing the list across threads is thus sound if `T` is `Sync`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for LinkedList<T> {}

// SAFETY: A `Cursor` is semantically a `&mut LinkedList<T>`, which is `Send`
// iff `LinkedList<T>: Send`, i.e. iff `T: Send`.
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for Cursor<'_, T> {}

// SAFETY: A `Cursor` is semantically a `&mut LinkedList<T>`, which is `Sync`
// iff `LinkedList<T>: Sync`, i.e. iff `T: Sync`. Moreover, all cursor methods
// require `&mut self`, so nothing can be done through `&Cursor`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for Cursor<'_, T> {}

// SAFETY: An `Iter` is semantically a `&LinkedList<T>` that only yields
// `&T`. It is `Send` iff `&T: Send`, i.e. iff `T: Sync`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Send for Iter<'_, T> {}

// SAFETY: An `Iter` is semantically a `&LinkedList<T>`, which is `Sync`
// iff `T: Sync`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for Iter<'_, T> {}

impl<T> LinkedList<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        LinkedList {
            head: None,
            tail: None,
            len: 0,
            _marker: PhantomData,
        }
    }

    // You may be wondering why it's necessary to have is_empty()
    // when it can easily be determined from len().
    // It's good custom to have both because len() can be expensive for some types,
    // whereas is_empty() is almost always cheap.
    // (Also ask yourself whether len() is expensive for LinkedList)
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.len == 0
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.len
    }

    /// Return a cursor positioned on the front element
    pub fn cursor_front(&mut self) -> Cursor<'_, T> {
        // Invariant 5 holds because of invariant 2.
        Cursor {
            current: self.head,
            list: self,
        }
    }

    /// Return a cursor positioned on the back element
    pub fn cursor_back(&mut self) -> Cursor<'_, T> {
        // Invariant 5 holds because of invariant 2.
        Cursor {
            current: self.tail,
            list: self,
        }
    }

    /// Return an iterator that moves from front to back
    pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, T> {
        // Invariant 6 holds: `head` is `None` or a live node of `self`,
        // which is borrowed immutably for the iterator's lifetime.
        Iter {
            next: self.head,
            _marker: PhantomData,
        }
    }
}

impl<T> Default for LinkedList<T> {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for LinkedList<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        // `take` frees each node exactly once and moves on to the next one.
        // This is iterative, so even huge lists can't overflow the stack.
        let mut cursor = self.cursor_front();
        while cursor.take().is_some() {}
    }
}

// the cursor is expected to act as if it is at the position of an element
// and it also has to work with and be able to insert into an empty list.
impl<T> Cursor<'_, T> {
    /// Take a mutable reference to the current element
    pub fn peek_mut(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        self.current.map(|node| {
            // SAFETY: By invariant 5, `node` points to a live node owned by
            // `self.list`. We hold the unique borrow of the list through
            // `&mut self`, and the returned reference borrows `self`, so no
            // other reference to this value can exist while it is alive.
            unsafe { &mut (*node.as_ptr()).value }
        })
    }

    /// Move one position forward (towards the back) and
    /// return a reference to the new position
    #[allow(clippy::should_implement_trait)]
    pub fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        let current = self.current?;
        // SAFETY: By invariant 5, `current` points to a live node of the
        // list. We only read its `next` link; no references into the node
        // are alive because they would have to borrow `self`.
        let next = unsafe { (*current.as_ptr()).next }?;
        // `next` is a live node of the list (invariant 4), so invariant 5
        // is maintained.
        self.current = Some(next);
        self.peek_mut()
    }

    /// Move one position backward (towards the front) and
    /// return a reference to the new position
    pub fn prev(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        let current = self.current?;
        // SAFETY: By invariant 5, `current` points to a live node of the
        // list. We only read its `prev` link; no references into the node
        // are alive because they would have to borrow `self`.
        let prev = unsafe { (*current.as_ptr()).prev }?;
        // `prev` is a live node of the list (invariant 4), so invariant 5
        // is maintained.
        self.current = Some(prev);
        self.peek_mut()
    }

    /// Remove and return the element at the current position and move the cursor
    /// to the neighboring element that's closest to the back. This can be
    /// either the next or previous position.
    pub fn take(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        let node_ptr = self.current?;
        // SAFETY: By invariants 1 and 5, `node_ptr` points to a live node
        // that was created with `Box::leak` and is owned by this list. We
        // reclaim ownership here and unlink it below, so it is no longer
        // reachable from the list and will not be freed a second time.
        let node = unsafe { Box::from_raw(node_ptr.as_ptr()) };
        let Node { value, prev, next } = *node;

        match prev {
            // SAFETY: `p` is a live node of the list distinct from the
            // removed one (no cycles, invariant 4). No references into it
            // exist since we hold `&mut self`.
            Some(p) => unsafe { (*p.as_ptr()).next = next },
            None => self.list.head = next,
        }
        match next {
            // SAFETY: `n` is a live node of the list distinct from the
            // removed one (no cycles, invariant 4). No references into it
            // exist since we hold `&mut self`.
            Some(n) => unsafe { (*n.as_ptr()).prev = prev },
            None => self.list.tail = prev,
        }
        self.list.len -= 1;

        // If both neighbours are `None`, the list is now empty, so
        // invariant 5 is maintained.
        self.current = next.or(prev);
        Some(value)
    }

    pub fn insert_after(&mut self, element: T) {
        match self.current {
            None => {
                // By invariant 5 the list is empty, so `None`/`None` are
                // the (adjacent) head and tail.
                // SAFETY: preconditions of `insert_between` are met as
                // described above.
                let node = unsafe { self.insert_between(element, None, None) };
                // The list is no longer empty, keep invariant 5.
                self.current = Some(node);
            }
            Some(current) => {
                // SAFETY: `current` is a live node of the list (invariant 5);
                // we only read its `next` link.
                let next = unsafe { (*current.as_ptr()).next };
                // SAFETY: `current` and `next` are adjacent in the list
                // (invariant 4, or `next` is `None` and `current` is the
                // tail by invariant 3).
                unsafe { self.insert_between(element, Some(current), next) };
            }
        }
    }

    pub fn insert_before(&mut self, element: T) {
        match self.current {
            None => {
                // By invariant 5 the list is empty, so `None`/`None` are
                // the (adjacent) head and tail.
                // SAFETY: preconditions of `insert_between` are met as
                // described above.
                let node = unsafe { self.insert_between(element, None, None) };
                // The list is no longer empty, keep invariant 5.
                self.current = Some(node);
            }
            Some(current) => {
                // SAFETY: `current` is a live node of the list (invariant 5);
                // we only read its `prev` link.
                let prev = unsafe { (*current.as_ptr()).prev };
                // SAFETY: `prev` and `current` are adjacent in the list
                // (invariant 4, or `prev` is `None` and `current` is the
                // head by invariant 3).
                unsafe { self.insert_between(element, prev, Some(current)) };
            }
        }
    }

    /// Allocates a new node containing `element` and links it into the list
    /// between `prev` and `next`. Returns a pointer to the new node.
    ///
    /// # Safety
    ///
    /// `prev` and `next` must be adjacent positions of `self.list`:
    /// - if `prev` is `Some(p)`, `p` must be a live node of `self.list` whose
    ///   `next` link equals `next`; if `prev` is `None`, `next` must equal
    ///   `self.list.head`.
    /// - if `next` is `Some(n)`, `n` must be a live node of `self.list` whose
    ///   `prev` link equals `prev`; if `next` is `None`, `prev` must equal
    ///   `self.list.tail`.
    ///
    /// (In particular, `None`/`None` is only valid for an empty list.)
    unsafe fn insert_between(&mut self, element: T, prev: Link<T>, next: Link<T>) -> NonNull<Node<T>> {
        // Allocation via `Box`, upholding invariant 1.
        let node = NonNull::from(Box::leak(Box::new(Node {
            value: element,
            prev,
            next,
        })));

        match prev {
            // SAFETY: by the caller's contract, `p` is a live node of the
            // list. No references into it exist, as we hold `&mut self`.
            Some(p) => unsafe { (*p.as_ptr()).next = Some(node) },
            None => self.list.head = Some(node),
        }
        match next {
            // SAFETY: by the caller's contract, `n` is a live node of the
            // list. No references into it exist, as we hold `&mut self`.
            Some(n) => unsafe { (*n.as_ptr()).prev = Some(node) },
            None => self.list.tail = Some(node),
        }
        self.list.len += 1;
        // Invariants 2, 3 and 4 hold again since the new node was placed
        // exactly between two adjacent positions.
        node
    }
}

impl<'a, T> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
    type Item = &'a T;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> {
        let node = self.next?;
        // SAFETY: By invariant 6, `node` points to a live node of a list
        // that is immutably borrowed for `'a`. Hence the node can neither be
        // freed nor mutated during `'a`, and handing out `&'a T` is sound.
        let node = unsafe { &*node.as_ptr() };
        // `node.next` is `None` or a live node of the same list
        // (invariant 4), so invariant 6 is maintained.
        self.next = node.next;
        Some(&node.value)
    }
}
```

I'll implement the list with `NonNull` pointers (covariant), a `PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>` for ownership, manual `Send`/`Sync`, a cursor holding an optional current node pointer, and `Drop`.

Invariants: `head`/`tail` are either both `None` (len == 0) or both point to valid heap nodes allocated via `Box`; every node's `prev`/`next` links are consistent; nodes are owned exclusively by the list.

